>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/training/">Workouts & Training</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Training</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill Walking With Arthritis</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Exercise is one of the few arthritis treatments that the major guidelines agree on, and walking is the simplest form of it. A treadmill makes walking predictable: a level surface, a speed you choose and a rail to hold. Here is how to use one without making your joints worse.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 17, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on published exercise guidance. General information, not medical advice.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#pain-rules" class="btn-ghost">How Much Pain Is OK?</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          When a joint aches, resting it feels like the obvious thing to do. For most people with
          osteoarthritis, the evidence points the other way: regular, sensibly dosed exercise
          tends to reduce pain and improve function, while inactivity weakens the muscles that
          protect the joint. The skill is in the dose — how fast, how long, how steep and how
          much discomfort to accept — and a treadmill lets you control every one of those
          numbers.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#evidence">What the Evidence Says About Walking and Arthritis</a></li>
            <li><a href="#why-treadmill">Why a Treadmill Suits Arthritic Joints (and Where It Does Not)</a></li>
            <li><a href="#pain-rules">How Much Pain Is Acceptable?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#session-length">How Long and How Often to Walk</a></li>
            <li><a href="#speed-incline">Speed and Incline Settings</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cushioning">Does a Cushioned Deck Help?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#shoes">Shoes and Insoles</a></li>
            <li><a href="#knee-vs-hip">Knee Versus Hip Arthritis on a Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#rheumatoid">Rheumatoid Arthritis and Flares</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-to-stop">When to Stop and See a Doctor</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes, for most people with knee or hip osteoarthritis a treadmill is a good
            place to walk, provided you start short and slow, keep the deck flat or nearly flat,
            avoid decline, and use a simple pain rule to judge how much is enough.</strong> The
            2019 American College of Rheumatology and Arthritis Foundation guideline strongly
            recommends exercise for knee, hip and hand osteoarthritis and lists walking among the
            suitable types. The 2019 Osteoarthritis Research Society International (OARSI)
            guideline makes structured land-based exercise a core treatment for knee and hip
            osteoarthritis.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start with 5 to 10 minutes</strong> at a comfortable speed, and add a few
            minutes a week.</li>
            <li><strong>Accept mild discomfort, not more.</strong> Pain up to about 5 out of 10
            during the walk is commonly treated as acceptable, as long as it settles by the next
            morning and is not creeping up week to week.</li>
            <li><strong>Walk flat or on a small incline</strong> (around 1 to 3 percent). Skip
            decline, which loads the front of the knee more.</li>
            <li><strong>Wear comfortable, cushioned walking shoes</strong>, and choose a machine
            with a cushioned deck and handrails if you are buying.</li>
            <li><strong>With rheumatoid or other inflammatory arthritis</strong>, ease right back
            during a flare and walk again once hot, swollen joints have settled.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you have a heart condition, poor balance, a recent joint operation or an
            inflammatory arthritis that is not yet under control, ask your doctor or physical
            therapist before starting. They can also set a starting dose specific to your joints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="evidence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Evidence Says About Walking and Arthritis</h2>
<p>
            The case for walking with arthritis rests on clinical guidelines rather than on one
            study, which is reassuring: the guideline panels weigh all of the trials together.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The ACR and Arthritis Foundation.</strong> The 2019 American College of
            Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation guideline for osteoarthritis of the hand, hip and
            knee, published in early 2020, strongly recommends exercise for all three. Exercise is
            the only non-drug treatment it strongly recommends for every one of those joints. The
            guideline does not rank one form above another: walking, strengthening, neuromuscular
            training and water-based exercise are all listed as options for knee and hip
            osteoarthritis, and it notes that outcomes tend to be better when exercise is
            supervised, at least at first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>OARSI.</strong> The 2019 OARSI guidelines for the non-surgical management of
            knee, hip and polyarticular osteoarthritis put arthritis education and structured
            land-based exercise programmes at the core of treatment for knee and hip
            osteoarthritis, with dietary weight management added for the knee. A structured
            walking programme on a treadmill fits that description.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The CDC.</strong> The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention advise that
            adults with arthritis, like all adults, aim for at least 150 minutes a week of
            moderate activity such as brisk walking, plus muscle strengthening on at least two
            days. Its arthritis pages are equally clear that you do not have to start there: begin
            slowly, pay attention to how your body responds, and know that even 5 or 10 minutes at
            a time counts. The CDC also recognises the Arthritis Foundation’s Walk With Ease
            programme, a six-week walking programme for people with arthritis that can be done in
            a group or on your own, and which has been reported to reduce pain and improve
            strength and balance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rheumatoid arthritis.</strong> The ACR’s 2022 guideline on exercise,
            rehabilitation and diet for rheumatoid arthritis strongly recommends consistent
            engagement in exercise over no exercise — the only recommendation in that guideline
            given at strong strength.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things the evidence does not say. It does not say walking rebuilds cartilage or
            reverses arthritis; the benefits are less pain, better function and the general
            health gains that come with being active. And it does not say every walk will feel
            good. Some increase in discomfort, especially early on, is normal, which is why the
            pain rules below matter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why a Treadmill Suits Arthritic Joints (and Where It Does Not)</h2>
<p>
            <a href="/is-walking-on-a-treadmill-the-same-as-walking-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Walking outside</a> is excellent exercise too, and if you enjoy it, keep doing it. The
            treadmill has some specific advantages for sore joints.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A level, predictable surface.</strong> No kerbs, camber, potholes or tree
            roots. Uneven ground makes the knee and ankle make small corrections with every step,
            and an unexpected dip on a painful knee is exactly what people with arthritis learn to
            dread.</li>
            <li><strong>Precise control of the dose.</strong> You can set the speed to a tenth of a
            mile per hour and the time to the minute, and repeat the session exactly. That makes
            the gradual build-up the guidelines describe much easier to follow.</li>
            <li><strong>No hills unless you choose them.</strong> Downhill walking is the part of
            many outdoor routes that hurts arthritic knees most. On a treadmill there is none.</li>
            <li><strong>You can stop at any minute.</strong> Outside, a walk that starts hurting at
            the halfway point still leaves you a mile from home. On a treadmill, you are never
            more than a few seconds from a chair.</li>
            <li><strong>Handrails.</strong> Light contact for balance is reasonable if your
            arthritis affects your confidence on your feet.</li>
            <li><strong>Weather.</strong> Many people with arthritis find cold, damp days the
            stiffest, and icy pavements are a fall risk. Indoors, the session goes ahead.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The drawbacks are real. The belt keeps moving when you want to stop, so you need a
            safe routine for getting off; there is a step up onto the deck, often higher than a
            stair; and walking at a fixed pace on a flat surface is repetitive, which can bother
            some joints over long sessions. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-on-and-off-a-treadmill-safely/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to get on and off a treadmill safely</a> covers the first two. Breaking a long walk into two shorter ones, or varying the speed
            a little, helps with the third.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pain-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Much Pain Is Acceptable?</h2>
<p>
            This is the question that stops most people with arthritis from exercising, so it is
            worth having a clear answer. Physical therapists commonly use a <strong>pain-monitoring
            model</strong>, developed by the Swedish physiotherapist Roland Thomeé for knee pain
            and since adapted for other conditions. Clinics vary in the exact thresholds, but a
            typical version goes like this.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Rate your pain from 0 to 10</strong>, where 0 is none and 10 is the worst
            imaginable. Note your usual level before you start.</li>
            <li><strong>During the walk, pain up to about 5 out of 10 is acceptable.</strong> Above
            that, slow down, shorten the session or stop.</li>
            <li><strong>After the walk, pain should settle back to your usual level by the next
            morning.</strong> If you wake up noticeably worse than before the session, it was too
            much; do less next time.</li>
            <li><strong>From week to week, your pain should not be trending upward.</strong> If it
            is, cut back the time or speed and build again more slowly.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Many people find their pain at the start of a walk is higher than in the middle: the
            joint is stiff, then warms up and eases. That pattern is common in osteoarthritis and
            is not a reason to stop. A useful habit is to note three numbers on your phone each
            session — pain before, worst pain during, pain the next morning — alongside the time
            and speed. After two or three weeks you will have a clear picture of what your joints
            tolerate.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some physical therapists use a stricter rule, especially soon after surgery or for
            people who are anxious about pain, and some use a “traffic light” version. If your
            therapist has given you a threshold, use theirs. The general point is the same:
            discomfort that settles is acceptable; discomfort that lingers and builds means the
            dose is too high.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pain is different from <strong>warning signs</strong>, which mean stop altogether:
            sharp, stabbing pain, a joint that locks or gives way, new swelling, or a joint that
            becomes hot and red. More on those in the section on when to stop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="session-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Long and How Often to Walk</h2>
<p>
            Start lower than you think you need to. A first session of 5 to 10 minutes at an easy
            pace is a perfectly good start, and if that leaves you sore the next day, drop to 5.
            The CDC’s advice that even 5 minutes counts is aimed precisely at people with
            arthritis who are worried about doing too much.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Build by small, regular steps.</strong> Add a couple of minutes per session
            each week if the pain rules are being met, and change only one thing at a time: time
            first, then speed, then a small incline. A typical progression might take someone
            from 10 minutes three times a week to 30 minutes most days over two to three months,
            but the right pace is the one your joints tolerate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Short sessions add up.</strong> Two 15-minute walks are often easier on an
            arthritic knee or hip than one 30-minute walk, and they count the same towards the
            weekly 150 minutes the CDC suggests aiming for in time. A 10-minute walk after each
            meal is a common pattern.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frequency beats intensity.</strong> Joints with osteoarthritis tend to like
            regular movement. Walking most days at a modest dose usually works better than a long,
            hard session twice a week followed by days of recovery. Many people also find that
            their joints feel stiffer after a few days off.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Add strength work.</strong> The guidelines recommend strengthening alongside
            aerobic exercise, and stronger thigh and hip muscles take load off the knee and hip.
            Simple sit-to-stands from a chair, step-ups and hip exercises, ideally taught by a
            physical therapist, complement the treadmill well. Our <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workout for seniors</a> and our guide to <a href="/how-long-should-seniors-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long seniors should walk on a treadmill</a> set out week-by-week build-ups that suit arthritic joints too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Speed and Incline Settings</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Speed.</strong> There is no correct speed for arthritis. Choose one at which
            you can walk with a normal, relaxed stride and hold a conversation. For many people
            with knee or hip osteoarthritis that is slower at first than the brisk 3 mph or so
            often quoted for healthy adults, and that is fine. Faster is not automatically better: if the pace forces you into a
            longer stride or a limp, slow down. Walking with a limp shifts load to other joints and
            is a sign the dose is too high. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-speed-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed for seniors</a> explains how to find a comfortable starting pace.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Short, quick steps are kinder than long ones.</strong> Over-striding — landing
            with your foot well ahead of your body — increases the braking force at the knee and
            pushes the hip into a larger range of movement. A slightly shorter, quicker step
            usually feels smoother on an arthritic joint.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline.</strong> A flat deck is a fine place to start and many people never
            need anything else. Once flat walking is comfortable, a small incline of around 1 to 3
            percent can add effort without adding speed, which some people with knee
            osteoarthritis prefer. Steep inclines are different: they increase how far the knee
            bends and how hard the thigh muscles work, and people with pain around the kneecap
            often find them aggravating. Hip arthritis can also complain on a steep gradient,
            because it asks for more hip flexion. Add incline in single-percent steps and drop it
            again if your pain score climbs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Decline.</strong> Avoid it. Walking downhill makes the thigh muscles work as
            brakes and loads the front of the knee more than level walking, which is why many
            people with knee arthritis find stairs and hills harder going down than coming up.
            Some treadmills offer a decline setting; leave it at zero. Our explainer on whether <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills are bad for your knees</a> goes into incline and decline in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Change things gradually during the session too.</strong> Start each walk with
            three to five minutes at a slow pace to let the joints loosen, and finish with a few
            minutes at the same slow pace before you stop the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cushioning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Does a Cushioned Deck Help?</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmills have some form of deck cushioning — elastomers, springs or flexible
            mounts between the deck and the frame — and manufacturers market it heavily. For
            people with arthritis it is worth having, with realistic expectations.
          </p>
          <p>
            A cushioned deck gives slightly under foot, which most people find more comfortable
            than concrete or asphalt. Comfort matters with arthritis: a walk that feels better is
            a walk you are more likely to repeat. What the marketing figures cannot tell you is how
            much a particular deck changes the load inside your knee or hip, and the forces in
            walking are far lower than in running to begin with. Shoes, speed, stride and session
            length make more difference than the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are buying, a well-cushioned deck, a belt at least 20 inches wide, sturdy
            handrails, a low step-up height and a slow starting speed are the features that matter
            for arthritis. A long belt matters less for walking than for running. Our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for bad knees</a> compares cushioning systems, and the <a href="/best-walking-machines-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking machines for seniors</a> covers lower, simpler machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walking pads are a reasonable option for short, slow walks, but most have thin decks
            with little cushioning and no handrail. If your balance or confidence is affected by
            arthritis, choose a model with a full handrail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shoes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Shoes and Insoles</h2>
<p>
            Wear comfortable, cushioned walking or <a href="/best-running-shoes-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>running shoes</a> with a firm heel counter and
            enough room in the toe box for any swelling or bunions. Comfort from the first step is
            a reasonable guide; there is no single shoe type proven best for arthritic knees or
            hips. Replace shoes once the midsole looks compressed or creased, since worn foam
            cushions less.
          </p>
          <p>
            Be cautious about specialist products. The 2019 ACR/Arthritis Foundation guideline
            conditionally recommends <em>against</em> lateral and medial wedged insoles and
            specially modified shoes for knee and hip osteoarthritis, because the evidence that
            they help is weak. Ordinary cushioned insoles for comfort are a different matter, and
            if foot pain or a foot deformity is part of the picture — common in rheumatoid
            arthritis — a podiatrist can advise on footwear and orthotics.
          </p>
          <p>
            Never walk on a motorised <a href="/can-you-walk-barefoot-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill in socks</a>, slippers or bare feet. Apart from the
            lack of support, a foot that slips on the side rail is a fall risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="knee-vs-hip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Knee Versus Hip Arthritis on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The general approach is the same for both, but the details differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Knee osteoarthritis.</strong> The knee usually tolerates level walking and
            gentle incline better than descent, stairs and deep bending. Keep decline at zero,
            keep the stride short, and watch for pain around or behind the kneecap on steeper
            gradients. Strengthening the thigh muscles, especially the quadriceps, is one of the
            most useful additions to treadmill walking for the knee. If the knee is swollen after
            a walk, reduce the time before reducing the speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hip osteoarthritis.</strong> The hip tends to dislike long strides and large
            ranges of movement, so a shorter step and a modest speed help. Many people with hip
            arthritis are stiffest first thing and after sitting; walking later in the day, after
            some gentle movement, is often more comfortable. Watch for pain in the groin, the
            front of the thigh or the buttock, and for a limp creeping in as the session goes on —
            a common sign to stop for the day.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>After a joint replacement</strong> the rules change. Timing, speed and step
            height are set by your surgeon and physical therapist, and the treadmill usually comes
            in some weeks after the operation. Our guides to the <a href="/treadmill-after-knee-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill after knee replacement</a> and <a href="/treadmill-after-hip-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill after hip replacement</a> cover that stage separately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rheumatoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Rheumatoid Arthritis and Flares</h2>
<p>
            Rheumatoid arthritis and other inflammatory types — psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing
            spondylitis, lupus-related arthritis — behave differently from osteoarthritis, and the
            pain rules need adjusting.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Between flares</strong>, walking is just as worthwhile. The ACR’s 2022
            guideline for rheumatoid arthritis strongly recommends consistent exercise, and
            regular aerobic activity also matters because rheumatoid arthritis raises the risk of
            heart disease. A treadmill’s level surface is useful if the feet and ankles are
            affected, which is common in rheumatoid arthritis.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>During a flare</strong>, when joints are hot, swollen and painful even at rest,
            the advice from arthritis charities and physical therapists is to ease right back:
            rest the inflamed joints, keep them moving gently through their range so they do not
            stiffen, and return to walking gradually once the swelling and heat have settled.
            Versus Arthritis, the UK charity, advises slow, gentle movement during a flare rather
            than stopping altogether, and warns against overdoing it. A flare is not the time to
            apply the 5-out-of-10 rule; walking on a hot, swollen knee or ankle is not
            recommended.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fatigue and morning stiffness</strong> are often as limiting as pain. Plan
            walks for the part of the day you feel best — for many people that is late morning or
            afternoon rather than first thing — and use short sessions on low-energy days rather
            than skipping them entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your rheumatologist or a physical therapist with rheumatology experience is the right
            person to set limits, particularly if your medication, joint damage or other
            conditions affect what is safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Stop and See a Doctor</h2>
<p>
            Ordinary arthritis aches that settle within a day are part of the process. These are
            not — stop the session, and get advice:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sharp, stabbing or sudden pain</strong> in a joint, as opposed to the
            familiar ache.</li>
            <li><strong>A joint that locks, catches or gives way</strong> under you.</li>
            <li><strong>New swelling, heat or redness</strong> in a joint, especially with a fever
            or feeling unwell. A hot, red, swollen joint needs prompt medical assessment.</li>
            <li><strong>Pain that is worse each day</strong> despite cutting back, or pain that
            wakes you at night when it did not before.</li>
            <li><strong>Calf pain and swelling</strong>, particularly after recent surgery or a
            long period of inactivity.</li>
            <li><strong>Chest pain or pressure, unusual breathlessness, dizziness or
            palpitations</strong> — stop at once, and seek urgent help if they do not settle.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            It is also worth seeing a physical therapist if you simply cannot find a dose that
            works — every session hurts, or you cannot get past a few minutes. Many people need
            a short period of supervised exercise, or treatment for an associated problem such
            as weak hip muscles, before walking becomes comfortable. And if walking itself is
            consistently painful despite all the adjustments here, a <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>recumbent bike</a>, an
            elliptical or water-based exercise may suit your joints better for a while.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are new to treadmills, our answer to <a href="/is-a-treadmill-good-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">is a treadmill good for seniors</a> covers who does well on one and who might prefer another machine.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Walking With Arthritis</h2>
          <p>Most setbacks come from getting the dose wrong rather than from walking itself.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Judging the session by how it felt during the walk</h3>
              <p>Arthritic joints often feel fine during a walk and complain that evening or the next morning. Use the next-morning pain score to set the following session, not how you felt at the time.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Adding time, speed and incline all at once</h3>
              <p>Three changes in one week make it impossible to tell which one caused a flare-up. Increase time first, then speed, then a small incline, one at a time.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Using decline or steep incline for variety</h3>
              <p>Decline loads the front of the knee more, and steep gradients can aggravate kneecap and hip pain. Keep decline at zero and incline to a few percent unless your therapist advises otherwise.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Walking through a hot, swollen joint</h3>
              <p>Heat, redness and new swelling are signs of inflammation, not stiffness. Stop, rest the joint, and seek medical advice if it does not settle quickly or you feel unwell.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The takeaway</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Treadmill walking is a sound choice for most people with knee
            or hip osteoarthritis, and exercise is recommended by the major arthritis
            guidelines.</strong> Start with 5 to 10 minutes at an easy pace on a flat deck, keep
            decline at zero, and build gradually, judging each step by whether pain settles by the
            next morning.
          </p>
          <p>
            With rheumatoid or other inflammatory arthritis, walk between flares and rest hot,
            swollen joints. Stop for sharp pain, locking, giving way or new swelling, and ask a
            doctor or physical therapist before starting if you have heart, balance or recent
            surgical concerns. If you are choosing a machine, our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best treadmills for bad knees</a> is the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

