        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill good for arthritis?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people with knee or hip osteoarthritis, yes. The 2019 ACR/Arthritis Foundation guideline strongly recommends exercise, including walking, and a treadmill gives a level surface, precise control of speed and time, and handrails. Start with 5 to 10 minutes at an easy pace, avoid decline, and build up gradually.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is walking on a treadmill good for knee osteoarthritis?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Generally yes. Guidelines from the ACR/Arthritis Foundation and OARSI put exercise at the centre of knee osteoarthritis treatment, and walking is one of the recommended forms. Keep the deck flat or on a small incline, avoid decline, take shorter steps, and judge the dose by how the knee feels the next morning.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much pain is OK when walking with arthritis?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A common physical therapy rule is that pain up to about 5 out of 10 during exercise is acceptable if it settles back to your usual level by the next morning and is not increasing week to week. Sharp pain, locking, giving way or new swelling are reasons to stop. If your therapist has set a different threshold, use theirs.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should you walk on an incline with arthritis?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A small incline of about 1 to 3 percent is fine for many people once flat walking is comfortable. Steep inclines can aggravate pain around the kneecap and in the hip, and decline walking loads the front of the knee more, so leave decline at zero.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should you walk on a treadmill with arthritis?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Start with 5 to 10 minutes, then add a couple of minutes per session each week if pain settles by the next morning. The CDC suggests working toward 150 minutes of moderate activity a week, and short sessions such as two 15-minute walks count the same as one longer walk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you walk on a treadmill during a rheumatoid arthritis flare?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually not on hot, swollen joints. During a flare, rest the inflamed joints, keep them moving gently through their range, and return to walking gradually once the swelling and heat have settled. Your rheumatologist or physical therapist can advise on your own limits.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

